Seven TypeScript features that many experienced developers still don’t use are const type parameters (5.0),` NoInfer` (5.4), assertion functions (3.7), labeled tuple elements (4.0), variance annotations (4.7), the` accessor` keyword (4.9), and` import type` combined with` verbatimModuleSyntax` (3.8 and 5.0).


If you learned TypeScript around 4.x and have been busy shipping since, you probably still write` as const` at call sites and add extra type parameters to stop inference from widening. Newer releases give you shorter ways to do both.


This article covers each feature in turn: what it does, which version introduced it, and one short example that compiles under strict mode.


## Key Takeaways


- A` const` type parameter (TypeScript 5.0) infers` \["/", "/users"\]` as` readonly \["/", "/users"\]` when the constraint is readonly, such as` readonly string\[\]` .
- ` NoInfer<T>` (TypeScript 5.4) stops a parameter from acting as an inference site, so a default value can’t widen the type that the other arguments establish.
- An assertion function written as` asserts value is T` narrows its argument for the rest of the scope after the call, not just inside an` if` block.
- Under` verbatimModuleSyntax` , an import is removed only when` type` applies to the whole statement.` import { type X } from "./mod"` emits` import {} from "./mod"` , so the module still runs.


