## Const Type Parameters (TypeScript 5.0)


[TypeScript 5.0 added the const modifier on type parameters](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-5-0.html) . It makes the compiler infer array and object literals as if the caller had written` as const` . Callers no longer need to remember the assertion to get literal types.


Pair the` const` modifier with a readonly constraint if you want readonly results. With a mutable constraint such as` string\[\]` , TypeScript 5.0 to 5.2 fell back to` string\[\]` . Since 5.3 the compiler infers a mutable tuple instead. The` const` modifier also only applies to literals written directly in the call. A variable declared earlier keeps the widened type it already had.


```text
function   defineRoutes  <  T   extends   readonly   string  []>(  paths  :   T  )  :   T   {   return   paths  ; }
const   a   =   defineRoutes  ([  "  /  "  ,   "  /users  "  ]);          // string[]


function   defineRoutesExact  <  const   T   extends   readonly   string  []>(  paths  :   T  )  :   T   {   return   paths  ; }
const   b   =   defineRoutesExact  ([  "  /  "  ,   "  /users  "  ]);     // readonly ["/", "/users"]


function   defineRoutesMutable  <  const   T   extends   string  []>(  paths  :   T  )  :   T   {   return   paths  ; }
const   c   =   defineRoutesMutable  ([  "  /  "  ,   "  /users  "  ]);   // ["/", "/users"] (TS 5.3+; string[] in 5.0–5.2)
```


## NoInfer for Controlled Inference (TypeScript 5.4)


[NoInfer<T> , added in TypeScript 5.4](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-5-4.html) , stops the compiler from using a given argument as an inference source. That argument is then checked against the type inferred from the other parameters, instead of widening it. Before 5.4, the usual workaround was a second type parameter constrained by the first. It worked, but it added a type parameter that appeared only once in the signature.


```text
function   createSelect  <  T   extends   string  >(  options  :   T  [],   initial  :   NoInfer  <  T  >) {
return   {   options  ,   value  :   initial   };
}


createSelect  ([  "  draft  "  ,   "  published  "  ],   "  draft  "  );      // OK
createSelect  ([  "  draft  "  ,   "  published  "  ],   "  archived  "  );
// Error: Argument of type '"archived"' is not assignable
// to parameter of type '"draft" | "published"'.
```


Without` NoInfer` ,` T` would widen to` "draft" | "published" | "archived"` and the typo would compile. The[utility types reference](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/utility-types.html#noinfertype) documents it alongside` Partial` and` Pick` .


## Assertion Functions (TypeScript 3.7)


An assertion function narrows its argument for the rest of the enclosing scope once it returns without throwing. That makes it a good fit for fail-fast guard clauses that don’t need a branch. A type guard (` value is T` ) narrows only inside the conditional that calls it. An` asserts value is T` signature,[introduced in TypeScript 3.7](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-3-7.html) , narrows everything after the call.


```text
function   assertIsDefined  <  T  >(  value  :   T  ,   name   =   "  value  "  )  :   asserts   value   is   NonNullable  <  T  > {
if (  value   ===   undefined   ||   value   ===   null  ) {
throw   new   Error  (  `${  name  }   is not defined  `  );
}
}


const   root   =   document  .  getElementById  (  "  app  "  );   // HTMLElement | null
assertIsDefined  (  root  ,   "  #app  "  );
root  .  classList  .  add  (  "  ready  "  );                   // root: HTMLElement
```


Assertion functions have one catch. If you write the helper as an arrow function assigned to an unannotated` const` , the call site fails with “Assertions require every name in the call target to be declared with an explicit type annotation.” Either use a function declaration or give the` const` an explicit type.


## Labeled Tuple Elements (TypeScript 4.0)


[Labeled tuple elements, added in TypeScript 4.0](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-0.html) , let you name each position in a tuple type. The names appear in editor hovers, signature help and error messages. Tuple labels change only what editors and error messages display. They don’t change the tuple’s type, and callers can destructure the elements under any names they like.[Since TypeScript 5.2](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-5-2.html) , a tuple can also mix labeled and unlabeled elements.


A hook-style return type is the classic use case:


```text
type   ToggleState   =   [  isOn  :   boolean  ,   toggle  :   ()   =>   void  ];


function   useToggle  (  initial   =   false  )  :   ToggleState   {
let   isOn   =   initial  ;
return   [  isOn  , ()   =>   {   isOn   =   !  isOn  ; }];
}


const   [  open  ,   toggleOpen  ]   =   useToggle  ();   // any names work; labels show in hovers
```


## Variance Annotations: in and out (TypeScript 4.7)


[Variance annotations, introduced in TypeScript 4.7](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-7.html) , let you declare explicitly whether a type parameter is covariant (` out` ), contravariant (` in` ), or both. Variance annotations are mostly for library authors. Application code almost never needs them. The 4.7 notes present them as a way to document intent and, for deeply recursive types, to speed up type-checking. TypeScript reports an error when an annotation contradicts how the parameter is actually used, but it accepts annotations that are stricter than necessary.


```text
interface   Producer  <  out   T  > {   get  :   ()   =>   T   }
interface   Consumer  <  in   T  > {   accept  :   (  value  :   T  )   =>   void   }


interface   Bad  <  in   T  > {   get  :   ()   =>   T   }
// Error: Type 'Bad<super-T>' is not assignable to type 'Bad<sub-T>'
// as implied by variance annotation.
```


## The accessor Keyword (TypeScript 4.9)


[TypeScript 4.9 added auto-accessors](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-4-9.html) . You declare one like a class field but prefix it with` accessor` . The compiler turns it into a getter and setter pair backed by an unreachable private field. An auto-accessor therefore gives a decorator a single place to intercept both reads and writes of the field.


The example below uses the[standard decorator signature from TypeScript 5.0](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-5-0.html#decorators) , with types from the compiler’s built-in[decorators.d.ts](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/main/tsc/internal/bundled/libs/lib.decorators.d.ts) . It requires` experimentalDecorators` to be off, because legacy decorators use a different signature.


```text
function   logged  <  This  ,   V  >(
target  :   ClassAccessorDecoratorTarget  <  This  ,   V  >,
context  :   ClassAccessorDecoratorContext  <  This  ,   V  >
)  :   ClassAccessorDecoratorResult  <  This  ,   V  > {
return   {
set  (  value  )   {
console  .  log  (  `  set   ${  String  (  context  .  name  )  }   =  `  ,   value  );
target  .  set  .  call  (  this  ,   value  );
},
};
}


class   Settings   {
@  logged   accessor   theme   =   "  light  "  ;
}
```


## import type with verbatimModuleSyntax (TypeScript 3.8 and 5.0)


Under[verbatimModuleSyntax](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#verbatimModuleSyntax) , TypeScript deletes an import only when` type` applies to the whole statement. It emits every other import as written. The[import type syntax dates back to TypeScript 3.8](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-3-8.html) .[The flag itself came in 5.0](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-5-0.html#--verbatimmodulesyntax) and replaced` importsNotUsedAsValues` and` preserveValueImports` , which were deprecated in 5.0 and no longer exist in TypeScript 7.


```text
{   "  compilerOptions  "  : {   "  verbatimModuleSyntax  "  :   true   } }
```


```text
import   type   {   User   }   from   "  ./api  "  ;           // erased entirely
import   {   fetchUser  ,   type   Post   }   from   "  ./api  "  ;   // emits: import { fetchUser } from "./api";
import   {   type   Config   }   from   "  ./config  "  ;      // emits: import {} from "./config";
```


The third line,` import { type Config }` , catches people out. An inline` type` modifier still leaves an empty import behind, so the module is loaded and its side effects run. When you turn the flag on, any type imported without a` type` modifier becomes a compile error. Migrating mostly means converting type-only named imports to` import type` .


